er ports.','Global `ip igmp snooping querier` is overridden by per-VLAN controls; EOS also needs a usable querier source address and VLAN/SVI conditions. Version 2 is the documented default.','Enabling the switch globally without a source address can still leave no useful queries. Lab: let memberships age without a router, then enable querier with source address and verify persistence.'),
('FRRouting (FRR)','PIM Source-Specific Multicast Range','https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/pim.html','Official FRRouting documentation — current 2026','FRR treats any group matching the SSM range—232.0.0.0/8 by default—as PIM-SSM regardless of the broader sparse/dense interface mode. Receiver signaling normally uses IGMPv3.','Build source-specific multicast without RP dependency and explicitly control which group range uses SSM semantics.','Receiver report creates source/group interest. FRR classifies the group against the SSM prefix list. A PIM Join is sent directly toward S according to RPF, and the multicast forwarding state is installed in the kernel.','`ip pim ssm prefix-list` changes the SSM range; `ip igmp` enables membership processing. `ip pim passive` suppresses PIM control traffic and is not a replacement for IGMP.','A receiver sending group-only semantics for an SSM group creates a signaling mismatch. Lab: customize the SSM range and compare state for a group before/after the change.'),
('FRRouting (FRR)','BGP Large Communities','https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/bgp.html','Official FRRouting documentation — current 2026','RFC 8092 Large Communities use three 32-bit values: Global Administrator and two local-data fields. They avoid classic community limitations with 32-bit ASNs and provide structured policy metadata.','Carry scalable routing policy metadata across modern BGP deployments.','UPDATE carries Large Community values. bgpd stores canonical ordered attributes. A large-community-list or route-map matches the entire string/set; route-map actions may overwrite or add values. Export sends the resulting attribute to the next peer.','FRR documents `show bgp large-community-list`, `show ip bgp large-community-info`, `match large-community ... [exact-match]`, and `set large-community ... [additive]`. Without `additive`, set replaces existing values.','Expanded large-community lists are for matching, not setting. Lab: add two values, use non-additive set, then additive set, and compare the exported attribute.'),
('FRRouting (FRR)','BGP Roles and Only-To-Customer','https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/bgp.html#bgp-roles-and-only-to-customers','Official FRRouting documentation — current 2026','RFC 9234 BGP Roles let peers identify provider/customer/peer/route-server relationships. The OTC attribute supports leak prevention by constraining where customer-only reachability can be propagated.','Make business/administrative relationship explicit and catch route leaks that ordinary AS_PATH loop detection cannot prevent.','BGP OPEN negotiates Role capability and validates compatible roles. UPDATE processing can add/check OTC according to relationship. Export policy then blocks propagation that would violate the role model.','FRR supports local-role values provider, customer, peer, rs-server and rs-client. Verify negotiated role in neighbor state and OTC on affected routes.','A role mismatch can fail early before normal UPDATE exchange. Lab: configure provider/customer correctly, then intentionally claim provider/provider and observe the negotiation result.'),
('Juniper Networks','BGP prefix-limit vs accepted-prefix-limit','https://www.juniper.net/documentation/us/en/software/junos/bgp/topics/topic-map/multiprotocol-bgp.html','Official Juniper Multiprotocol BGP documentation — current 2026','`prefix-limit` counts received prefixes before import policy; `accepted-prefix-limit` counts routes that survive policy. Newer Junos can teardown, drop-excess or hide-excess depending on the option.','Protect memory/control-plane scale at the exact stage that matters: raw peer input versus accepted RIB candidates.','Peer sends NLRI. The received counter is checked against prefix-limit. Import policy accepts/rejects. Accepted counter is checked against accepted-prefix-limit. The configured action then logs, tears down, drops or hides excess routes.','`show bgp neighbor` exposes relevant counts and newer dropped/hidden state. Use raw received limits when the peer itself is the scale risk; use accepted limit when policy-filtered scale is the concern.','A raw limit can reset a peer that sends many routes even if policy discards most. Lab: send 1,000 routes, accept 50, and compare both counters/actions.'),
('Juniper Networks','Ethernet OAM Link Fault Management','https://www.juniper.net/documentation/us/en/software/junos/network-mgmt/topics/concept/lfm-ethernet-oam-ex-series-understanding.html','Official Juniper IEEE 802.3ah OAM documentation — current 2026','Ethernet OAM Link Fault Management exchanges OAM PDUs between point-to-point peers. Active mode initiates discovery; passive waits. Periodic OAM and event TLVs monitor link health beyond simple carrier.','Detect degraded, asymmetric or error-prone Ethernet links and support standardized operations/remote-loopback workflows.','OAM is enabled. An active endpoint initiates discovery; peers exchange mode/capability information. Once operational, periodic PDUs monitor liveness and event thresholds can generate TLVs. Missed-PDU thresholds change the OAM session even while PHY carrier remains Up.','Verify active/passive mode, discovery state, interval and missed-PDU threshold. Physical Up is not proof that the OAM peer is healthy.','Passive/passive can fail to initiate. Lab: active/passive, then passive/passive, then simulate OAM loss while retaining carrier.'),
('Juniper Networks','LACP Fast vs Slow Periodic Negotiation','https://www.juniper.net/documentation/us/en/software/junos/fusion-enterprise/topics/task/interfaces-configuring-aggregated-ethernet-lacp-l2.html','Official Juniper LACP documentation — current','Fast periodic is about one LACPDU per second; slow is about every 30 seconds. Actor and partner advertise timeout expectations, and the transmitter honors the receiver’s requested rate.','Choose a convergence/control-overhead tradeoff appropriate to critical aggregated links.','Each side advertises LACP actor/partner state including timeout preference. The peer transmits according to the receiver’s requested cadence. Eligible members reach collecting/distributing; protocol timeout can remove a failed member before upper-layer protocols react.','Configure `periodic fast|slow` under aggregated Ethernet LACP and inspect both actor and partner state. Local configuration alone is not proof of effective negotiated cadence.','Fast/slow mismatch does not mean each side blindly transmits at its own interval. Lab: mismatch settings and capture LACPDUs to measure actual behavior.'),
('Aruba/HPE','MVRP Dynamic VLAN Registration','https://www.arubanetworks.com/techdocs/AOS-CX/10.13/PDF/l2_bridging_6300-6400.pdf','Official Aruba AOS-CX Layer-2 Bridging Guide — 10.13','MVRP dynamically propagates VLAN registration attributes between bridges. Join registers VLANs; Leave and LeaveAll help remove registrations no longer needed.','Reduce manual VLAN provisioning across Layer-2 domains while controlling which interfaces may dynamically register/deregister VLANs.','An MVRP participant advertises a Join. Peers dynamically register the VLAN and propagate it where appropriate. Leave requests deregistration. LeaveAll starts a refresh cycle; still-valid registrations are reasserted before the Leave timer expires.','Use `mvrp`, `mvrp registration normal|fixed|forbidden`, timers, `show mvrp config`, `show mvrp state`, and `show mvrp statistics`. MVRP and VLAN translation cannot coexist on one interface.','Inconsistent timers can create churn; fixed/forbidden modes change dynamic behavior. Lab: dynamically create a VLAN across three switches, then withdraw the last registration and observe deletion.'),
('Aruba/HPE','VSF Split Detection','https://www.arubanetworks.com/techdocs/AOS-CX/10.13/PDF/vsf.pdf','Official Aruba AOS-CX VSF Guide — 10.13','A VSF stack can partition into independent fragments after enough VSF link failures. Split detection provides an independent way for fragments to discover each other and suppress one side.','Prevent split-brain duplicate forwarding when conductor and standby end up in separate active fragments.','A VSF link failure partitions the stack. Each fragment has role state. Split-detect connectivity reveals the competing fragment. The fragment with the primary stays active; the other shuts non-VSF front-plane interfaces. Reconnection restores one stack.','Verify ring/chain topology, conductor/standby placement and split-detect path. A ring degrading to one connected chain is not yet a split brain.','Without split detection, conductor and promoted standby fragments can both forward. Lab: split a four-member stack with conductor/standby separated and compare front-plane state with split detection.'),
('Aruba/HPE','OSPF Passive Interface Semantics','https://www.arubanetworks.com/techdocs/AOS-CX/10.13/PDF/ip_route_8400.pdf','Official Aruba AOS-CX IP Routing Guide — 10.13','A passive OSPF interface stays in the OSPF process and its connected network can still be advertised, but it sends/receives no OSPF packets and forms no adjacency on that interface.','Advertise user/loopback subnets without accidental neighbors or unnecessary Hello traffic on endpoint-facing VLANs.','The interface is placed in an area and contributes its connected prefix. `ip ospf passive` suppresses Hello/DD/LSR/LSU adjacency exchange on that link. Other routers can still learn the subnet through the originating router’s LSAs.','Verify the interface is passive and verify the prefix appears remotely. No neighbor on a passive interface is expected; on a transit link it is a configuration error.','Making an inter-router transit interface passive prevents adjacency. Lab: compare desired passive user SVI with an accidentally passive routed inter-switch VLAN.')]
assert len(L)==21

# one unique, feature-specific question per current lesson
Q=[]
def add(v,d,typ,mech,stem,choices,ans,why,miss,src,kind='new today'):
    Q.append(dict(id=len(Q)+1,vendor=v,domain=d,type=typ,mechanism_fingerprint=mech,stem=stem,choices=choices,correct=ans,rationale=why,miss=miss,source=src,kind=kind))
U={t:u for v,t,u,*_ in L}
current=[
('Cisco Systems','OSPFv3 Prefix Suppression','architecture','C_OSPFV3_PREFIX_LSA','After global OSPFv3 prefix suppression, neighbors remain Full and SPF still uses a transit link. Which remote database result proves suppression is working?',['The transit address is absent from prefix-bearing LSAs while Router/Network-LSA topology remains','The Router-LSA for the local router disappears','The transit prefix is converted to a Type-5 external LSA','All loopback prefixes disappear with the transit prefix'],0,'OSPFv3 can preserve the topology graph while omitting eligible locally configured prefixes from Intra-Area-Prefix/link advertisement.','Separate topology advertisement from address-prefix advertisement.'),
('Cisco Systems','EIGRP Wide Metrics and RIB Scaling','command/output','C_EIGRP_RIB_SCALE','A change to `metric rib-scale` is proposed on named EIGRP. What operational effect should the change plan explicitly account for?',['EIGRP routes are cleared and reinstalled in the RIB with newly scaled metric values','Every neighbor must renegotiate K values','The process falls back to classic 32-bit metrics','Only feasible-successor routes are removed'],0,'Cisco documents route replacement in the RIB when RIB scale changes.','RIB scaling changes representation/installation and can cause route churn.'),
('Cisco Systems','BGP Route Flap Dampening Penalty and Reuse','state machine','C_BGP_DAMP_REUSE','A route is currently damped with a penalty above the suppress threshold. Without manual clearing, what makes it usable again?',['Penalty decay takes it below the reuse threshold','The neighbor sends a Route Refresh','MED becomes lower than the current path','The peer completes a TCP keepalive exchange'],0,'Dampening is a penalty state machine: reuse occurs after the decayed penalty crosses the reuse threshold.','Do not substitute ordinary BGP session or path-selection events for the dampening state transition.'),
('Palo Alto Networks','PIM Source-Specific Multicast','packet reasoning','P_SSM_IGMPV3','A receiver wants only source 10.1.1.10 for group 232.1.1.1. Which host-to-firewall signaling contains the source identity needed for SSM?',['An IGMPv3 source-specific membership report','An IGMPv2 group-only report','A PIM Register from the receiver','A BGP multicast UPDATE'],0,'IGMPv3 can encode source-specific membership, which lets the receiver-side router build (S,G) state.','SSM requires source-aware receiver signaling; group-only ASM signaling is insufficient.'),
('Palo Alto Networks','BGP Route Dampening Profiles','configuration review','P_DAMP_PROFILE_ATTACH','A Dampening Profile has the desired suppress/reuse values, but a peer’s flapping route never accumulates dampening state. What should be verified first?',['The peer or peer group actually references the Dampening Profile','The peer has a lower MED than competitors','The OSPF area range is advertised','ECMP Symmetric Return is enabled'],0,'A profile object changes nothing until it is attached through peer/peer-group configuration.','Distinguish object definition from feature attachment/inheritance.'),
('Palo Alto Networks','OSPF Area Range Summarization and Suppression','architecture','P_OSPF_AREA_RANGE','An ABR has four /24 intra-area routes and an Area Range /22 with Advertise disabled. What should routers in another area learn from that range?',['No inter-area advertisement matching that configured range','One /22 summary plus all four /24s','A Type-5 external /22','A default route generated by the DR'],0,'Disabling Advertise on the Area Range suppresses the matching inter-area advertisement.','Area Range is an ABR inter-area control, not an external-route or DR function.'),
('Fortinet','BGP Route Tags Driving SD-WAN Rules','control-plane sequence','F_ROUTE_TAG','A BGP route arrives with community 30:5, but an SD-WAN rule matches route-tag 12. Which FortiGate step creates the value the SD-WAN service consumes?',['An inbound route-map matching the route and applying `set-route-tag 12`','Changing the BGP community itself to 12:12','Adding an OSPF tag to the interface','Setting member cost to 12'],0,'FortiGate route-map policy translates route attributes/matches into the internal route tag used by SD-WAN.','Community metadata and FortiGate route-tag are different objects joined by policy.'),
('Fortinet','BGP Neighbor Groups with Multiple Remote AS Values','configuration review','F_REMOTE_AS_FILTER','A neighbor group already has `remote-as 65010`. You need `